    {
      "id": "h5kjorg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Universal Law of Gravitation states that gravitational force is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between two masses.",
        "Mass is scalar and remains constant everywhere, whereas Weight is a vector quantity that varies with local acceleration due to gravity (g).",
        "Archimedes' Principle states that the upward buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid displaced by a submerged body.",
        "Kepler's Third Law specifies that the square of orbital period (T²) is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis (r³).",
        "Escape velocity is independent of the mass of the escaping body and equals √2 times the orbital velocity."
      ],
      "id": "eblfmbd"
    },
